    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle, we need to consider the properties of a right triangle. In a right triangle, the sum of the squares of the two legs is equal to the square of the hypotenuse. In this problem, we have a right triangle with one side of length 6 cm and one side of length 8 cm. The third side, which we will call \\( c \\), is the remaining side we need to find.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, the relationship between the sides of the right triangle is given by:\n\\[ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 \\]\nwhere \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the legs of the triangle, and \\( c \\) is the hypotenuse. In this case, we can identify \\( a = 6 \\) cm, \\( b = 8 \\) cm, and we need to find \\( c \\). Substituting the known values into the Pythagorean theorem, we get:\n\\[ 6^2 + 8^2 = c^2 \\]\n\\[ 36 + 64 = c^2 \\]\n\\[ 100 = c^2 \\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we find:\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{100} \\]\n\\[ c = 10 \\]\nTherefore, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is:\n\\[ \\boxed{10.00} \\]",
