    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given a right triangle with one side measuring 6 cm and another side measuring 8 cm, we aim to find the length of the remaining side. In a right triangle, the greatest side is opposite the right angle, so we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the unknown side.\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. Let's denote the unknown side as \\( c \\).\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[ c^2 = 6^2 + 8^2 \\]\n\nFirst, calculate the squares of the known sides:\n\\[ 6^2 = 36 \\]\n\\[ 8^2 = 64 \\]\n\nNow, add these values together:\n\\[ c^2 = 36 + 64 \\]\n\\[ c^2 = 100 \\]\n\nTo find \\( c \\), take the square root of both sides:\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{100} \\]\n\\[ c = 10 \\]\n\nThus, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is \\(\\boxed{10.00}\\) cm.",
